From 44cd4e091bcb866b87326b1caf9fb2c0b5e9a995 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 19:15:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Measure item 1-1's remaining half, and remove the walk it
 found repeated

The roadmap sizes what is left of item 1-1 (lazy function compilation) as "the parse
and the expression-tree construction are still eager, and on real source that is the
larger half". That share was derived rather than measured -- the ceiling table's
92-96%, minus the emission A/B's 17-36%, read off as the remainder -- which is the move
performance-roadmap.md 3.5 has a rule about and item 3-6 paid for once already.

Two instruments take it directly.

--compile-phases splits parse / expression-tree construction / emission on the six real
corpora rather than on --compile-scaling's synthetic declaration walls, against the same
body-free control, with the closure rewrite as its own column and the end-to-end compile
as a decomposition check. Deferral on, i.e. the engine as it ships: parse 9.4-13.5%,
tree construction 33.6-63.9%, emission 25-57%. Tree construction is the single largest
phase on five of six corpora, parse and tree together are 43-75% of compile, and the
parse -- the part an early-error rule forbids deferring -- is a tenth of the whole. The
end-to-end column is taken FIRST for a reason: every compile here registers a deferred
site per relayed lambda under a GCHandle that is never freed, so taken last it read 3.4x
the sum of the phases on Box2D and 1.0x on jQuery, which is exactly how many sites a
deferred compile of each registers (982 against 1).

--defer-population counts what the ceiling table never could. Item 1-1 already registers
a deferred site per nested lambda and forces it on first invocation, both once per site,
so registered - forced is exactly the functions a front-end deferral would never have had
to look at. After evaluating each corpus the way its harness does -- which for CodeLoad
is the whole benchmark -- 84-99.7% of a script's functions are never invoked: jQuery 347
of 415, Closure 46 of 51, Box2D 877 of 978, PdfJS 699 of 804, Typescript 1339 of 1574,
Mandreel 2689 of 2697.

Together those say the remaining half is worth 43-75% of compile over a population that
is almost entirely never needed. They also correct item 1-1's own ceiling table:
--compile-profile stubs outermost function bodies and jQuery has exactly one, the IIFE
the library is written inside (99.91% of its bytes), so its control is an empty file and
the recorded "96.5% of its compile in bodies that are never called" is everything except
the parse -- of a body CodeLoad calls first.

Measuring the phases then found a repeat inside the half that already landed.
LambdaRewriter.Rewrite descends through nested lambdas, because CheckForClosure threads a
capture up the whole lambda chain and can only do that from a walk that sees the chain.
RuntimeMethodBuilder.Relay called it again with the relayed lambda as its own root, so a
lambda at depth d was walked d+1 times -- and with item 1-1's deferral on, relaying
jQuery's single IIFE walked the entire program at the moment of deferring it.

Counted rather than argued, and then counted again for the claim that matters. Every
relay in a real compile is a repeat: 0 needing a rewrite of their own against 415, 51,
978, 804 and 1574 that had already had one. And with the switch off, so the repeat still
runs, it creates 0 captures the first walk had not -- the direct instrument, because
"every relay is a repeat" and "the repeat does nothing" are two claims and the first
counter only measures one of them.

Relay now skips a lambda a descending walk has already entered, marked on the lambda
itself. The mark is set only by a walk that rewrites nested lambdas, so RewriteRootOnly --
the async pre-rewrite, which stops at each nested lambda by design -- leaves it clear, and
anything built after that walk (a generator or async body rewritten into a state machine)
is rewritten at relay exactly as before.

Whole compile 0.782x on jQuery and 0.867x on Typescript, six of six ABBA pairs each, one
corpus per process. Box2D does not separate on that instrument and its control arm's own
spread is 55.6%, so the changed phase was measured directly instead: emission
99.9 -> 54.8 ms (0.549x) and whole compile 267.4 -> 207.2 ms (0.775x), in the round where
--compile-phases' built-in parse control held to 6%. The round where that control moved
1.5-1.9x is discarded on that basis.

BROILER_JS_RELAY_REWRITE_ONCE=0 restores the repeat. RelayRewriteTests adds 19 cases --
transitive capture through three levels, a write back through one, per-instance loop cells
two levels down, a generator and an async body nested in a closure, `this` through an
arrow, a named function expression's own name, direct eval two levels down, five levels
reading and writing every level, and the counter invariant -- each asserted on both
settings of the switch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
